// SSH channels — three hazards that are all invisible in the common case, and a rationale I checked.
//
// `packages/ssh/src/channel.wac` is 233 lines and its header is a list of ways to be wrong that no
// test would catch. Read together they have one shape:
//
//   > **The two channel numbers.** Each side picks its own number for the same channel and they need
//   > not agree … a client that echoes back its own number addresses a channel the server may not
//   > have. **With a single channel numbered zero on both sides, which is the common case, that
//   > mistake works perfectly.**
//
//   > **Flow control is not optional.** A client that never adjusts reads exactly one window of
//   > output and then hangs forever, having done nothing wrong that any error would report. Since the
//   > default window is large, **that is invisible for short commands and a deadlock for long ones.**
//
//   > **Exit status arrives as a request, not a reply.** It is not a response to anything, and
//   > nothing prompts it.
//
// Two of the three fail *only* under conditions a test rarely creates — more than one channel, more
// than one window of output. That is the interesting property: these are not edge cases at the
// boundary of a value's range, they are the ordinary case at a scale nobody reached.

import { Result, Bytes } from "core";

/**
 * A parsed channel message.
 *
 * **The shipped type is one struct for all eleven**, and it is the sharpest instance of the
 * closed-set question in the tree:
 *
 *     export struct Incoming {
 *       i32 kind;           // the message number, or 0 when this is not a channel message
 *       i32 channel;        // our number
 *       i32 senderChannel;  // OPEN_CONFIRMATION: the number the server uses. Nothing else sets it.
 *       i32 window;         // OPEN_CONFIRMATION: their initial window. WINDOW_ADJUST: the increment.
 *       i32 extra;          // OPEN_CONFIRMATION: max packet. EXTENDED_DATA: type code. REQUEST: exit code.
 *       u8[] data;          // DATA: the bytes. REQUEST: the request name. FAILURE: the description.
 *       bool ok;            // false when the message was truncated or malformed
 *     }
 *
 * `extra` means three different things and `data` means three different things, each selected by
 * `kind`, and nothing relates them. Two fields carrying six meanings is `../../../QUESTIONS.md`'s
 * *paired values with nothing holding the pair* at its largest, and unlike the generated tables it is
 * not a performance trade — the union lowering makes an enum **one wasm struct with a tag and a slot
 * per payload field of every variant**, which is what this struct already is, by hand, without the
 * tag being checked.
 */
export enum Incoming {
  Open(u32 theirChannel, u32 initialWindow, u32 maxPacket),
  OpenConfirmation(Ours ours, Theirs theirs, u32 initialWindow, u32 maxPacket),
  OpenFailure(u32 reason, Bytes description),
  WindowAdjust(Ours ours, u32 increment),
  Data(Ours ours, Bytes bytes),
  ExtendedData(Ours ours, u32 typeCode, Bytes bytes),
  Eof(Ours ours),
  Close(Ours ours),
  Request(Ours ours, Bytes name, bool wantReply, Bytes payload),
  Success(Ours ours),
  Failure(Ours ours),
}

/**
 * Our number for a channel.
 *
 * **Not `Theirs`, and that is the whole point.** The shipped struct has `i32 channel` and
 * `i32 senderChannel` four lines apart, distinguished by a doc comment, and the header explains that
 * sending the wrong one addresses a channel the peer may not have. It then says the part that makes
 * this worth a type rather than a comment: *"With a single channel numbered zero on both sides, which
 * is the common case, that mistake works perfectly."*
 *
 * So the defect is undetectable until a program opens a second channel, at which point it is a
 * message delivered to the wrong place. Fourth instance here of **two values of one primitive type
 * with different meanings** — after `tor`'s RSA and ed25519 identities, `fmt`'s two `FixedBig`
 * capacities and `git`'s two varints — and the first where the failure needs *concurrency* to appear.
 */
export struct Ours { u32 of; }

/** The peer's number for the same channel. */
export struct Theirs { u32 of; }

export Result<Incoming, Malformed> parse(Bytes packet) { … }

/** Not a channel message at all — a transport message arriving here is ordinary, not an error. */
export struct NotOurs { u32 kind; }

/** The message was truncated or did not parse. */
export struct Malformed { u32 kind; string why; }

// ## What could not be written
//
// ### The stated reason for the flat struct is wrong, and the real one is narrower
//
// The shipped comment is explicit about why it is not an enum:
//
//   > One struct for all of them, with the fields each kind happens to use. **A tagged union per
//   > message would be tidier and would not survive the bindgen boundary, where this has to arrive as
//   > numbers and byte arrays anyway.**
//
// Checked against `packages/wacc/src/bindgen.wac`, which is the generator in question. It emits *"a
// class per struct **and enum**"*, and for an enum:
//
//   - a class holding the WasmGC reference — *"Nothing is copied: `$ref` is the WasmGC reference
//     itself, and every accessor calls back into the module"*;
//   - **a static constructor per variant**, `static Data(…)` calling `$bind$e_…_Data_new`;
//   - a `tag`, typed in TypeScript as a **string union** of the variant names, with a lookup table.
//
// So a tagged union crosses intact, by reference, with a *better* discriminator than `i32 kind` — a
// string union a TypeScript checker can make exhaustive. Nothing has to arrive as numbers and byte
// arrays.
//
// **What bindgen does not emit is a way to read a variant's payload.** Field accessors and `$of` are
// generated inside `if (!isEnum)`; the enum branch generates constructors and the tag and stops. That
// is because wac itself has no field access on a variant outside `match`, so there is nothing for the
// generator to mirror.
//
// And for `Incoming` that gap is exactly the one that matters: this value is **produced by wac and
// consumed by a host**, so construction is the direction bindgen supports and reading is the
// direction it needs. The author reached the right conclusion for their file from a reason that is
// false about the mechanism — **the third time today the named blocker was not the blocker**, after
// `atof.wac`'s *"wac has no generics"* and `kinds.wac`'s *"wac has no module-level constants"*.
//
// The ask that follows is small and precise, and it is not *"support enums in bindgen"*: it is **a
// generated accessor per variant payload**, or equivalently a way for a host to perform the match
// that wac performs. `../../tls/src/client.wac` left open whether its `u8[]` connection state existed
// because of a real boundary constraint or because nobody re-examined it. **This answers it**: the
// constraint is real, it is one-directional, and it is narrower than either file assumed.
//
// ### An obligation, which no type here has ever been asked to carry
//
// Flow control is the first thing in this directory that is neither a *state* nor a *set*. A channel
// does not require the caller to be in a phase or to pick from a closed list — it requires them to
// **keep doing something**: send `WINDOW_ADJUST` as output is consumed, forever, or the connection
// stops with no error anywhere.
//
// Every device this directory has proposed describes a value. An enum names which of several a value
// is; a wrapper says what it means; a union says how it failed. None of them says *you must call this
// again*. That is a session type or a linear obligation, it is a much larger language than anything
// else on this list, and the honest position is that this file **names the gap and does not propose
// the feature** — the same discipline `../../crypto`'s note takes with `leaks(…)`.
//
// What is worth recording precisely is the shape of the failure, because it is what makes the ask
// non-academic: **there is no error.** A deadlock is the absence of an event, so no `Result`, no
// fault union and no exhaustive match can be made to fire. Every other finding here is about a wrong
// value reaching a caller; this is about a right value never arriving.
//
// ### And `kind == 0` is a correct sentinel, in a struct built out of incorrect ones
//
// `Incoming.kind` uses `0` for *this is not a channel message*, and zero is not a valid SSH message
// number, so the range excludes it. The shipped `parse` also documents why the case is not an error —
// *"a transport message arriving here is ordinary, not an error, and the caller decides what to do
// with it."* Both halves of the rule, in a struct whose other six fields are the counterexample.
//
// This directory has now found thirteen sentinels and four that clear the bar, and all four are in
// files whose authors wrote down the disjointness argument. That is the pattern rather than the
// count: **the sound ones are sound because someone checked, and the unsound ones are in the places
// nobody had a reason to look.**
